CH = dict(num=8, title="Life Processes in Animals", theme="Digestion, absorption, breathing, respiration, circulation",
intro="Animals take in food, break it down, get energy from it, breathe and transport materials around the body. These essential activities, nutrition, circulation, respiration, excretion and reproduction, are the life processes. This chapter studies in detail how humans digest food (nutrition) and breathe and respire, and then compares other animals such as ruminants, birds, fish, frogs and earthworms.",
big_questions=["Why does chapati taste sweet if chewed long?", "Where is digested food absorbed?", "How is breathing different from respiration?", "How do fish, frogs and earthworms breathe?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "9.0 Introduction"),
("p", "Life processes (nutrition, circulation, respiration, excretion, reproduction) are needed for survival. Animals eat many kinds of food: bees and sunbirds suck nectar, infants drink milk, pythons swallow prey whole, some aquatic animals filter tiny food particles. Food gives **energy** for all life processes."),
("p", "Food contains complex components (**carbohydrates, proteins, fats**) that must be broken into simpler forms. This happens in the **alimentary canal**, a long tube from the **mouth to the anus**. Digestive juices secreted along it break food down; the simple forms are absorbed and carried to the body."),
("h2", "9.1 Nutrition in Animals"),
("h3", "9.1.1 Digestion in human beings"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Digestion** is the breakdown of complex food components into simpler forms in the body. **Mechanical digestion** is the initial breakdown of food into fine pieces by teeth through crushing and chewing."),
("h3", "Mouth cavity"),
("ul", ["Teeth do mechanical digestion; the tongue mixes food with **saliva**; saliva moistens food, making it soft and easy to swallow.", "Saliva contains a digestive juice that breaks **starch into sugar**. This is why chapati or rice tastes sweet if chewed for 30–60 s. Food is partly digested in the mouth.", "**Oral hygiene:** brush teeth and clean the tongue twice a day, rinse after every meal to prevent decay and bad smell."]),
("steps", [("Set up (Activity 9.1)", "Label test tubes A and B. Put a teaspoon of boiled rice in A and a teaspoon of rice chewed for 30–60 s in B. Add 3–4 mL water to each."), ("Add iodine", "Add 3–4 drops of iodine solution to each and mix."), ("Observe", "A turns **blue-black** (starch present). B shows no change or only light blue-black (starch has been broken into sugars by saliva).")]),
("p", "Iodine gives a blue-black colour with starch. Chewing for longer should reduce the colour further."),
("h3", "Food pipe (oesophagus)"),
("p", "A long flexible tube from mouth to stomach. Its walls **contract and relax in wave-like motion** to push food down. Such movement occurs along the entire alimentary canal."),
("h3", "Stomach"),
("ul", ["Walls contract and relax to **churn** food, which mixes with secretions from the stomach lining: **digestive juice, acid and mucus**.", "Digestive juice breaks down **proteins**; **acid** helps in this and kills many harmful bacteria; **mucus** protects the stomach lining from the acid.", "Food becomes a **semi-liquid mass**, partially digested.", "**Fascinating fact:** in 1822 Alexis St. Martin was shot in the stomach and his wound left a permanent opening, which let Dr William Beaumont observe digestion directly and study how foods and emotions affect it."]),
("h3", "Small intestine"),
("ul", ["About **6 m long**, the longest part of the alimentary canal; called 'small' because it is narrow.", "Receives secretions from **three sources**: its own lining, the **liver** and the **pancreas**.", "**Liver:** makes **bile**, mildly basic; it neutralises acid coming from the stomach and breaks fats into tiny droplets.", "**Pancreas:** makes **pancreatic juice**, basic, which neutralises acid and breaks down carbohydrates, proteins and fats.", "Juice from the small intestine wall further breaks down fats, proteins and partly digested carbohydrates.", "**Absorption:** digested nutrients pass into blood vessels in the intestinal walls. The inner lining has thousands of **finger-like projections (villi)** that increase surface area for absorption.", "Blood carries nutrients for energy, growth and repair.", "**Celiac disease:** the body reacts to **gluten** (a protein in wheat, barley, rye), damaging the intestinal lining. It is managed by avoiding gluten; millets (jowar, bajra, ragi) are naturally gluten-free."]),
("h3", "Large intestine"),
("ul", ["About **1.5 m long** but **wider** than the small intestine.", "Absorbs **water and some salts** from the undigested food, making the waste semi-solid **stool**.", "Stool is stored in the **rectum** and expelled through the **anus**, a process called **egestion**.", "Fibre-rich foods (fruits, vegetables, whole grains) make stool easier to pass.", "Helpful bacteria in the large intestine break down undigested food (especially fibre) and make useful nutrients. Fermented foods (curd, buttermilk, shrikhand, kanji, pickles, gundruk, poita bhat) support gut health.", "The *Charaka Samhita* stresses easily digestible food and spices like ginger, black pepper and cumin; modern advice: eat on time, eat mindfully, avoid overeating."]),
("table", ["Part of alimentary canal", "Key function"], [["Mouth", "Chewing (mechanical), saliva acts on starch"], ["Oesophagus", "Moves food to stomach by wave-like contractions"], ["Stomach", "Churns food; acid, mucus, juice digest proteins; semi-liquid mass"], ["Small intestine", "Bile, pancreatic and intestinal juices complete digestion; **absorption** into blood"], ["Large intestine", "Absorbs water and salts; forms stool"], ["Rectum and anus", "Storage and egestion of stool"], ["Liver, pancreas (associated organs)", "Make bile and pancreatic juice"]]),
("h3", "9.1.2 Do all animals digest food the same way?"),
("ul", ["**Birds** have no teeth; they have a muscular **gizzard** that grinds food with the help of swallowed grit (small stones).", "**Ruminants** (cows, buffaloes, goats) partly chew grass and swallow it into the stomach (rumen), where it is partially digested. The food is brought back to the mouth and chewed thoroughly: **rumination**. A cow chews about 8 hours a day.", "Alimentary canals vary in structure and function to suit different foods."]),
("h2", "9.2 Respiration in Animals"),
("p", "Some nutrients build and repair the body; others, like sugar, are broken down to release **energy**. The process of converting nutrients into usable energy is **respiration**."),
("h3", "9.2.1 Respiration in humans"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Breathing** is the process of inhaling and exhaling air. The **respiratory system** carries out breathing and gas exchange."),
("p", "Without food we can live for weeks and without water for a day or two, but without breathing only for a few minutes."),
("p", "**Path of air:** nostrils &rarr; nasal passages (hair and mucus trap dust, so breathe through the nose) &rarr; windpipe &rarr; two branches into the two lungs &rarr; smaller branches ending in balloon-like **alveoli**. The lungs are protected by the **rib cage**; below them is the dome-shaped muscle, the **diaphragm**."),
("ul", ["**Science and Society:** small infectious particles can get past the filters; the SARS-CoV-2 virus of COVID-19 affected the respiratory system."]),
("h3", "Mechanism of breathing"),
("table", ["", "Inhalation (breathing in)", "Exhalation (breathing out)"], [["Ribs", "Move up and outwards", "Move down and inwards"], ["Diaphragm", "Moves **downwards**", "Moves **upwards**"], ["Chest space", "Increases", "Decreases"], ["Air", "Drawn into lungs", "Pushed out of lungs"]]),
("p", "**Model (Activity 9.2):** a bottle with its base removed, a Y-tube with two balloons in the neck and a rubber sheet at the base. Pulling the sheet down inflates the balloons (inhalation); releasing it deflates them (exhalation). **Balloons = lungs; rubber sheet = diaphragm.**"),
("ul", ["Breathing practices: Pranayama, Ladakh's Tummo breathing and deep breathing help respiratory health, relaxation and concentration."]),
("h3", "What do we breathe out? (Activity 9.3, teacher demonstration)"),
("p", "Equal fresh lime water in tubes A and B. Pass ordinary air with a pichkari into A, blow exhaled air into B with a straw. Only **B turns milky**, so exhaled air contains more carbon dioxide."),
("table", ["Gas", "Inhaled air", "Exhaled air"], [["Oxygen", "About 21%", "About 16–17%"], ["Carbon dioxide", "About 0.04%", "About 4–5%"]]),
("p", "Not all oxygen is used up in the lungs; some animals use a larger fraction."),
("h3", "Gas exchange in alveoli"),
("p", "Alveoli have thin walls surrounded by fine blood vessels. **Oxygen** passes from alveoli into blood and is carried to all body parts; **carbon dioxide** from the body is carried by blood to the alveoli and released into the air to be breathed out."),
("h3", "Respiration: using oxygen to release energy"),
("note", "FORMULA", "Glucose + Oxygen &rarr; Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy"),
("table", ["Breathing", "Respiration"], [["Taking in air and giving out air", "Using oxygen to break down glucose and release energy"], ["**Physical** process", "**Chemical** process"], ["Happens in the lungs/respiratory system", "Happens inside body cells"], ["Brings in oxygen, removes carbon dioxide", "Gives energy for walking, running, playing, thinking"]]),
("h3", "Circulatory system"),
("p", "Made of the **heart, blood and blood vessels**. The heart pumps blood through the vessels to carry nutrients, oxygen and other substances to all parts and carry away waste."),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Smoking** damages lungs, causes persistent cough, frequent infections and lung cancer. **Passive smoking** (inhaling others' smoke) is especially dangerous for children, pregnant women and the elderly."),
("h3", "9.2.2 Breathing in other animals"),
("table", ["Animal", "Breathing organ"], [["Birds, elephants, lions, cows, goats, lizards, snakes", "Lungs (structure differs)"], ["Fish and most aquatic animals", "**Gills**, rich in blood vessels; exchange gases dissolved in water"], ["Frog tadpole", "Gills"], ["Adult frog", "Lungs on land and **skin** in water"], ["Earthworm", "Moist **skin**"]]),
("h2", "Likely exam questions"),
("qa", [("What are life processes?", "Processes essential for survival: nutrition, circulation, respiration, excretion, reproduction."),
("What is the alimentary canal?", "Long tube from mouth to anus in which food is digested and absorbed."),
("Why does chapati taste sweet on chewing?", "Saliva breaks starch into sugar."),
("What does iodine test show in the saliva experiment?", "Rice alone turns blue-black (starch); chewed rice shows little or no colour as saliva has digested starch."),
("What are the functions of acid, mucus and juice in the stomach?", "Juice breaks proteins; acid helps and kills bacteria; mucus protects stomach lining."),
("Why is the small intestine called small, and what are its functions?", "It is narrow although 6 m long; it completes digestion and absorbs nutrients through villi."),
("What do the liver and pancreas contribute?", "Liver gives bile (neutralises acid, breaks fats); pancreas gives pancreatic juice (neutralises acid, digests carbohydrates, proteins, fats)."),
("What happens in the large intestine?", "Water and salts are absorbed and stool is formed and stored in the rectum before egestion."),
("What is rumination? Name some ruminants.", "Bringing back partly digested food for thorough chewing; cow, buffalo, goat."),
("How do birds grind food without teeth?", "Using the gizzard and swallowed grit."),
("Trace the path of air to the alveoli.", "Nostrils, nasal passages, windpipe, bronchial branches in lungs, alveoli."),
("What happens to the ribs and diaphragm in inhalation and exhalation?", "Inhalation: ribs up and out, diaphragm down. Exhalation: ribs down and in, diaphragm up."),
("What do the balloons and rubber sheet represent in the model?", "Lungs and diaphragm."),
("Differentiate breathing and respiration.", "Breathing is physical, exchange of air; respiration is chemical, releasing energy from glucose with oxygen."),
("Write the word equation for respiration.", "Glucose + Oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy."),
("How do fish, frogs and earthworms breathe?", "Gills; lungs/skin (tadpoles gills); moist skin.")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["Report on traditional oral hygiene practices.", "Make a 3-D clay model of the digestive system with labels.", "Find out what AQI is and how air quality affects farmers, factory workers and street vendors.", "Learn about box-breathing and its benefits; think about how birds cope with low oxygen at high altitude."]),
],
takeaways=["Digestion breaks food into simple forms in the **alimentary canal**: mouth &rarr; oesophagus &rarr; stomach &rarr; small intestine &rarr; large intestine &rarr; anus.", "**Saliva** digests starch; **stomach** juice digests protein; **bile, pancreatic and intestinal juices** complete digestion in the small intestine.", "**Absorption** occurs in the **small intestine**; the large intestine absorbs water and salts.", "Ruminants chew cud; birds use a gizzard.", "Breathing: ribs and **diaphragm** change chest volume; gas exchange occurs in **alveoli**.", "**Respiration:** glucose + oxygen &rarr; carbon dioxide + water + energy; breathing is physical, respiration chemical.", "The **circulatory system** (heart, blood, vessels) transports nutrients and oxygen.", "Fish use gills, frogs use skin and lungs, earthworms use moist skin."])
